<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anastasio Somoza Debayle was the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somoza-family">Somoza family</a> dictators of Nicaragua, commanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> and serving as president in 1967–1972 and 1974–1979. His regime's corruption, notably the misuse of aid after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> earthquake of 1972, and the murder of Pedro Joaquín Chamorro in 1978 fuelled a national insurrection led by the Sandinistas. He fled in July 1979 and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, in 1980.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle.jpg" alt="Anastasio Somoza Debayle" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anastasio_Somoza_Debayle.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anastasio Somoza Debayle, nicknamed Tachito, was born on 5 December 1925, the third child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a> and Salvadora Debayle. From the age of ten he was educated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with his elder brother Luis, and he graduated from the United States Military Academy at West Point on 6 June 1946. His father, who was president of Nicaragua from 1937 to 1947 and again from 1950, made him chief of staff of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> on his return, which made him head of the armed forces. After their father's assassination on 21 September 1956 Luis became president, and Anastasio helped ensure that presidents loyal to the family held office until 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected president on 5 February 1967, Somoza took office on 1 May. Luis had ruled more gently than their father, but Anastasio shared his father's intolerance of dissent, and his rule soon resembled it, with harsh repression. Barred from immediate re-election, he arranged for a three-man junta to hold office from 1972 while he kept command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> and so stayed the de facto ruler. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> earthquake on 23 December 1972 killed about 5,000 people and virtually destroyed the city. Martial law was declared and Somoza took formal control as head of the National Emergency Committee. He reportedly embezzled much of the money sent from abroad to rebuild the city.</p><p>He was re-elected in 1974, by which time the Catholic Church had begun to speak out against his government. In 1975 he began a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a>, targeting people suspected of supporting it, and human rights groups condemned his record by the late 1970s. Support for the Sandinistas grew, especially after the earthquake and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a> withdrew its support, partly over the murder of the American journalist Bill Stewart by government soldiers. The newspaper publisher Pedro Chamorro, an opponent of the Somozas, was assassinated on 10 January 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 17 July 1979 Somoza resigned and fled to Miami, taking with him, it is claimed, much of the national treasure; the Sandinistas found less than two million dollars in the treasury, and the country's foreign debt stood at 1.6 billion dollars. Refused asylum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, he took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and bought a house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>. A Sandinista commando team ambushed his car there on 17 September 1980 with machine guns and an RPG-7 rocket, and killed him and two others. He was buried in Miami. A Brazilian newspaper estimated in 1979 that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somoza-family">Somoza family</a>'s fortune stood between 2 and 4 billion dollars. His memoirs, Nicaragua Betrayed, blamed the Carter administration for his downfall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anastasio Somoza Debayle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the National Guard, had Sandino murdered in February 1934, seized power in 1936 and ruled until he was assassinated in León in 1956; his sons Luis Somoza Debayle and <b>Anastasio Somoza Debayle</b> continued the dynasty until 1979, governing through the Guard, the Liberal Nationalist Party and pliant presidents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Two days after <b>Anastasio Somoza Debayle</b> fled, Sandinista forces entered Managua, ending the Somoza family's 43-year rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in León, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.44,-86.88&amp;year=1960" aria-label="León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2164 1700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anastasio Somoza Debayle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2614 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2614 1970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.44,-86.88&amp;year=1960">What was here in 1960? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
